  "response": "Before an unusual grammatical feature shared by two languages can be credited to a common ancestor, the competing explanations have to be cleared away one by one, and each of them \u2014 inheritance, borrowing, universal tendency, accident \u2014 carries its own evidentiary burden. Chance resemblance is one of the explanations investigators must exclude before positing descent. The comparative method establishes genetic relationship through systematic sound correspondences across many lexical items. It is able to do so because basic vocabulary such as numerals and kinship terms is comparatively resistant to borrowing; that resistance is what yields the stable correspondence sets the method requires. Shared irregularities in inflectional paradigms are treated as strong evidence of common descent, since arbitrary morphological residue is improbable as an independent innovation.\n\nA shared typological profile is by itself sufficient proof of genetic relatedness. Such sufficiency cannot be squared with the comparative method's insistence that relationship be demonstrated through systematic sound correspondences across many lexical items; the two standards of proof cannot both govern the practice. Grammatical features are never transferred between languages in situations of contact \u2014 a blanket exclusion that cannot be reconciled with what prolonged bilingualism demonstrably does to grammatical structure.\n\nGrammatical patterns can be transferred between languages through prolonged bilingualism, and the accumulation of such transfers across a region has produced the Sprachbund, a geographic zone in which neighbouring languages converge on shared structural features. Geographic contiguity among the languages sharing a feature supports a contact explanation. The Balkans confirm this contiguity-based reasoning: Balkan languages from different Indo-European branches share a postposed definite article. The distribution of that article across unrelated branches also indicates that the postposed definite article of the Balkan languages is reconstructed for Proto-Indo-European.\n\nRetroflex consonants in Indo-Aryan languages, by contrast, have been attributed to a Dravidian substrate \u2014 an attribution that supplies evidence for the bilingual transmission of grammatical structure sketched above. Such substrate proposals were in circulation long before the field was equipped with explicit methodological controls on contact explanations. Thomason and Kaufman set out explicit criteria for establishing contact-induced change, and as a result documented historical evidence of bilingual communities strengthens a contact hypothesis. What tips the balance in an actual dispute is rarely a single feature, but the alignment of the sound correspondences, the morphological irregularities and the social history of the speech communities behind one hypothesis and not the other.",
